Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.958
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.650
NICOLAS DE CRISTOFARO:
Welcome to NET204--

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.420
Connecting Your Data
Center to Cloud--

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.382
Approach for Mission
Critical Network Reliability.

00:00:13.382 --> 00:00:14.590
My name's Nick De Cristofaro.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:16.780
I'm a customer engineer
here at Google,

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.320
specializing in
Google Cloud Network.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.065
So first, let's talk
about Google's network.

00:00:25.065 --> 00:00:26.690
So when we talk about
Google's network,

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.610
we're talking about a
massive global platform

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:29.300
as you can see--

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.370
134 points of presence, massive
investments in sub-sea cables,

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.340
connecting the world, as
you can see behind me,

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.130
all within a private
backbone network that we own

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.740
or that we have private
connectivity for.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.050
The map here clearly
distincts some regions

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.300
that we have, of course also
future regions that we're

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.010
planning to launch, as well
as the mass point of presence

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.730
that we have to connecting
your data center to the cloud.

00:00:56.555 --> 00:00:57.930
So let's talk a
bit about how you

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.050
connect Google to the cloud.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.300
So connecting your
place to our place--

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.960
we actually have five ways of
connecting Google to the cloud.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.330
We divide these into
two different sections,

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.640
public addressing and
private addressing.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.730
The latter at the top is the
public addressing method,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.590
which is actually
connecting Google

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.500
via various means of peering.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.150
So direct peering
is one of them,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.843
which offers the ability to
connect to Google via standard

00:01:23.843 --> 00:01:26.010
peering that you would do
with an internet exchange,

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.810
essentially connecting
your public prefixes--

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.530
so public address blocks
that you would own--

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.070
to Google Cloud via
standard BGP peering.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.610
On the top right-hand side,
we talk about carrier peering.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.500
Carrier peering is
essentially the same means

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.010
of connecting to Google via
public addressing, however

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.930
through a carrier that
provides that connectivity.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.480
So this is in case,
for example, you

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.730
do not have the connectivity
to Google directly

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.160
to be able to meet us at
our peering facilities,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.740
you can do that
through a carrier.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.440
On the bottom part, we speak
about private addressing.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.900
Now, this is really interesting
for enterprise environments

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.442
where you want to
extend connectivity

00:02:02.442 --> 00:02:06.090
from your on-premise environment
using private RFC 1918

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.580
addressing and connecting
directly to Google Cloud.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.970
So that means being
able to reach directly

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.770
your on-prem workloads
to Google Cloud via two

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.230
means of connectivity.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.670
And then of course, we have
the VPN option, which kind of

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.300
sits hybrid in the middle, which
provides a mix of connectivity

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.450
across public internet links
to do the private addressing,

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.905
extending your on-prem
environment to the cloud.

00:02:26.905 --> 00:02:28.530
So when we speak of
private addressing,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.140
we actually have two
methods of doing this.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.940
Once it's dedicated
interconnect,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.570
which is to directly connect
to Google Cloud via one

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.400
of our various
points of presence,

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.272
that means your
equipment is co-located

00:02:40.272 --> 00:02:41.730
in a point of
presence, or you have

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.650
connectivity to our
point of presence

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.745
and establishing a
dedicated link to us.

00:02:46.745 --> 00:02:48.120
The partner
interconnect offering

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.360
is actually to provide
connectivity, again, privately,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.610
however, through a partner,
a service provider,

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.110
that is able to
extend the last mile

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.450
connectivity from your
on-premise environment

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:58.890
to Google Cloud.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.810
In case you do not have
connectivity in one

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.310
of our points of
presence, you could

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.350
use a partner interconnect
to do that for you.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.110
And as a hybrid
model, as I mentioned,

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.140
VPN is a way to connect
via public internet links.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.820
And often, enterprises
start that way

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.190
before actually moving on
to purchase a dedicated

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:15.828
or a partner
interconnect offering.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.370
And I will show you
later on actually

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.540
how seamlessly the transition is
done between a VPN connectivity

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.075
to Google Cloud
through a partner

00:03:23.075 --> 00:03:24.450
or a dedicated
interconnect link.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.550
So let's speak a bit about
the Cloud Interconnect.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:32.260
But first, I'm
pleased to announce

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.523
that we are announcing 100
gig connections at our POPs.

00:03:35.523 --> 00:03:37.690
So now you're able to connect
not only with 10 gigs,

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:42.830
but now with 100 gig connections
at our points of presence.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.660
So Cloud Interconnect,
as I mentioned,

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:47.990
is a method to provide
access to private RFC 1918

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:50.260
addressing from your
on-prem workloads

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.360
directly to the cloud.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.890
And it enables essentially
a hybrid cloud connectivity

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:54.910
model.

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:58.305
It does not require the use
of management of hardware VPN

00:03:58.305 --> 00:04:00.430
devices because this is
something that you directly

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:04.420
connect your on-premise router
to us via the various POP

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.160
locations that we're located in.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.200
So from the left hand side here,
we see dedicated interconnect,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.160
as I mentioned.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.120
We could do 10 gigs.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.370
Now we can do 100 gigs.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:15.340
We can provide an LACP bundle,
so a link group bundle,

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.550
from 8 times 10 gigs or 2 times
100 gigs [? per ?] provide

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:21.970
and transport circuits directly
to our cloud environment

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.430
from your on-premise
data center.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.950
And a partner interconnect
provides dedicated bandwidth

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.830
for sub-circuit rates,
starting at 50 megabits

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.810
all the way to 10 gigs provided
through a service provider

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:33.310
network.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.720
So here's the bit about how the
interconnect deployment looks

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:41.360
like.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.960
So we can look at a dedicated
interconnect in this way.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.025
We have a co-location
facility in the middle.

00:04:46.025 --> 00:04:47.650
The co-location
facility is essentially

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.408
where you would meet us at
one of our points of presence.

00:04:50.408 --> 00:04:52.450
So you may either have a
cage already established

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:53.290
in one of the facilities.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.082
For example, some of
the Equinix facilities

00:04:55.082 --> 00:04:57.130
is a very common
co-location facility

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.140
or any other
co-location facilities.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:03.130
Or you can extend connectivity
there through any kind

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.870
of telco providers
that can extend

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.940
that connectivity to you.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.370
And from that, you extend
your on-premise connection

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.890
to your data center, and
we provide connectivity

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:12.960
to our cloud network.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.420
Now, here is a list of our
interconnect locations.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.660
So as you can see, we're
present all around the world

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.180
in all the continents,
except, of course, Antarctica.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:26.450
And then we have connectivity
with low latency links, which

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.860
is the dark green circles,
which are providing

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.040
very little [INAUDIBLE] access
to Google Cloud resources

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.852
if you need low latency
access between your workloads

00:05:37.852 --> 00:05:38.560
and Google Cloud.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.690
So that means you can
provide sub 5 milliseconds

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:44.780
on average from these sites to
any regions that are located

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.160
in these points of presence.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.570
Looking at the partner
interconnect offering,

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:53.180
this is essentially
a model where

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.940
the partner is essentially
providing the connection

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:58.400
to Google, so partners
have established a partner

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.980
relationship with us already.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:01.760
They've established
connectivity with us.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.800
And they provide that last mile
connectivity to their clients.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.690
So various partners offer that.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.510
And if you're not
looking for something

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:10.550
at 10 gigs or potentially
higher throughput,

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.800
you would look at a partner
to offer that because they

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.750
can provide sub-circuit
rates from 50 megabits

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:16.740
all the way up to 10 gigs.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.130
So they can provide that
solution and also one thing

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.720
that some partners
are able to offer

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.593
as well is that they
can minimize the router

00:06:22.593 --> 00:06:23.135
requirements.

00:06:23.135 --> 00:06:26.837
So you may not need a router
at the co-location facility

00:06:26.837 --> 00:06:28.670
themselves, in case you
want to do something

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:29.910
like a multi-cloud
scenario and you

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:31.202
want to connect cloud to cloud.

00:06:31.202 --> 00:06:34.400
Some partners offer the ability
to connect two clouds together

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.440
via virtual router offering.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.390
So let's look at some of the
distinction between partner

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:43.937
and dedicated interconnect.

00:06:43.937 --> 00:06:45.270
So why would you choose partner?

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:46.830
Why would you choose dedicated?

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.150
I talked to a lot of customers.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.070
Depending on their use
case, it's very clear.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.740
Partner is where you're
not able to have access

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:54.060
to our points of presence.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:58.140
You're not physically co-located
in a point of presence.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.510
You may not need to have
a requirement of 10 gigs.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.093
You don't need 10 gigs, which
is the minimum that we can offer

00:07:03.093 --> 00:07:04.380
with dedicated interconnect.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:05.950
Therefore, a partner
will provide you

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.340
a sub circuit rate starting
from 50 megabits all

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:09.480
the way to 10 gigs.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.660
And we can provide that via
VLAN attachments on our end.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.880
You pay only for what you need.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.820
SLAs require at least to have
two VLAN attachments, which

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.070
would terminate at two
different availability

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:21.515
zones within a metro area.

00:07:21.515 --> 00:07:23.640
If you want to have an SLA
of three nines, and then

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.807
four nines, you would just
establish that across two

00:07:25.807 --> 00:07:27.810
different metropolitan areas.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.240
You can actually also establish
with partner interconnect,

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.040
you don't need to
be within one org.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.807
We can actually extend the
connectivity from multiple orgs

00:07:34.807 --> 00:07:36.390
because the partner
interconnect VLANs

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:38.220
can land in different
organizational units

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.640
within GCP.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.548
For dedicated, it's
often used by customers

00:07:43.548 --> 00:07:44.840
that require a high throughput.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.150
They require a
minimum of 10 gigs.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.060
The customer would essentially
meet us at one of our POPs

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.160
or again, extend connectivity
to one of our POPs

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:54.260
either via service
provider or meet

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:56.900
us physically in one of
our points of presence.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:58.550
All the VLANs are
actually writing

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:00.080
on the same physical
link, so that

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:02.060
means that when you
establish VLAN attachments

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:05.108
through our Cloud console,
you would actually

00:08:05.108 --> 00:08:06.650
establish the VLAN
attachments within

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:10.190
the same physical circuit
that's established with us.

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:13.250
SLAs, again, require at
least two connections

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.300
to one metro area across
two availability zones.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.780
And then all VLANs need to land
in the same organizational unit

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.640
in GCP for dedicated
interconnect.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.270
That means that you can
establish a project in GCP

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.352
within one org.

00:08:27.352 --> 00:08:29.060
And that project can
share its resources,

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.100
but it's still bound
by the organization.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:32.929
That's one of the advantages
that the partner interconnect

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:34.640
can do, is you can
actually span that

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.590
across multiple
organization if you need to.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:42.080
So let's talk about
a few different ways

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:43.663
that you can actually
use interconnect

00:08:43.663 --> 00:08:48.210
and some of the use cases that
our customers are using today.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.690
So here's just an example,
again, of the private routing

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:55.370
that you can establish between
your on-premise infrastructure

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:57.260
and our cloud environment.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.480
So essentially, you would
establish a BGP peer

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:02.360
via our offering, which is
called a Cloud Router, which

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.550
would be deployed
in the cloud region,

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.640
and then establish BGP peering
with your on-premise router

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:08.780
and exchanging prefixes.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.390
In this case here, we have a
headquarter with an external

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:17.330
data center that has a
network of 192.168.55.0/24.

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:20.150
And we're advertising
that to the cloud region,

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:23.000
and the cloud region's
receiving that prefix over BGP

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:27.260
in the cloud region
as 192.168.49.0/24.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:29.000
And we're advertising
that across.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.650
So we can establish
that connectivity

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.590
through the Cloud Router,
which is our virtual BGP

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.250
speaker in the cloud and
your on-premise BGP speaker.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:41.590
Another use case
that we've seen is

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:43.607
because of the concept
of global routing

00:09:43.607 --> 00:09:45.190
and the concept of
our global network,

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.530
we're essentially able
to deploy within one VPC

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:48.700
multiple regions.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:51.190
So that actually allows you
to connect with one router

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:53.650
that you deploy in one region.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:56.560
You can actually reach natively
other regions within GCP.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.960
So that means that we're able
to advertise prefixes that

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.910
would exist, let's say,
in a different region

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.130
than the region where the
Cloud Router is deployed.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:05.800
And you can actually ride the
backbone of Google's network

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:07.930
to provide connectivity
between these regions.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:09.640
You do not need to
duplicate your effort

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.020
in building separate
Cloud Router

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:12.910
instances in different regions.

00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:15.600
This is done natively through
our global backbone network.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.100
It's a flag that's enabled in
Cloud Router when you actually

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:18.700
deploy this.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:19.825
It's called global routing.

00:10:19.825 --> 00:10:22.780
It enables you to seamlessly
transition our network

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:25.598
and allow access
to remote regions

00:10:25.598 --> 00:10:26.890
from the Cloud Router's region.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.810
DNS is another one of our very
popular solutions as well.

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.600
Customers would like
to deploy on-premise.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.635
They have on-premise
already at a DNS server

00:10:38.635 --> 00:10:40.260
where they have post
names that they're

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:44.190
resolving for their
private resources on-prem.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:46.590
And then they also want to
deploy resources in the cloud,

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:49.170
so they would use
Cloud DNS in the cloud,

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:52.662
specify specific A
records for different VMs

00:10:52.662 --> 00:10:54.120
that they spin up
within the cloud,

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.120
and they would want their
on-premise environment

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.320
to also resolve host
names in the cloud.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.070
So they would do that
via DNS forwarding.

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:02.140
We have both inbound and
outbound DNS forwarding,

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:04.260
which actually allows
you to resolve host names

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:06.660
from on-premise
workloads to the cloud

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.027
and also from the cloud
to on-premise workloads.

00:11:09.027 --> 00:11:11.360
And we can allow that through
the means of interconnect.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.540
So via either a dedicated
or partner interconnect

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:15.180
or through VPN,
we're able to extend

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.838
that connectivity via the
means of DNS forwarding.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:24.437
Another great offering is
through the shared VPC model,

00:11:24.437 --> 00:11:26.270
so within Google Cloud,
we're able to deploy

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:29.570
a model via shared VPC, which
essentially structures a host

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.880
project, which is essentially
the anchor for all

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.950
your networking resources
within the cloud.

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:36.410
So service projects do not
need to worry about networking.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:37.935
They just need to
consume resources.

00:11:37.935 --> 00:11:39.560
They can be development
teams that just

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:40.727
want to deploy applications.

00:11:40.727 --> 00:11:43.018
They don't need to know about
the underlying networking

00:11:43.018 --> 00:11:46.130
infrastructure, the routing, the
subnets, the firewall policies.

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:48.470
This is all done by a
centralized networking project

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:50.428
that would be managed by
generally a networking

00:11:50.428 --> 00:11:52.040
team within your organization.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:55.490
And that project would deploy
Cloud Router within a region.

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:57.440
And in that Cloud Router,
it could essentially

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:59.120
share the resources
of the interconnect

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:00.620
with the individual
service project.

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:03.590
So the service project would
consume networking resources

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:07.160
similar to subnets that you
would grant them access to.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.890
And it can natively route
with any routing that's

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:11.780
available within
the host project

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.888
to your on-premise environment.

00:12:13.888 --> 00:12:15.680
So again, this really
simplifies the model.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.830
You don't need to duplicate
multiple interconnections

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:19.795
in each project.

00:12:19.795 --> 00:12:21.170
You can actually
centralize this,

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:22.712
have a central
network administration

00:12:22.712 --> 00:12:25.400
team that manages the
networking for your environment.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.860
And the rest of the teams that
need to manage development

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:30.050
projects or other types
of service projects

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:31.730
do not need to worry
about networking.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:33.350
And they're essentially
connected back

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:36.110
to your on-premise environment
via policies that you set.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:42.820
Another use case is connecting
privately to Google services.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.490
Google has many APIs
that can be exposed--

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:48.000
for example, Cloud Storage
or BigQuery-- that you may

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.680
want to utilize from on-premise.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:52.370
And also, because you have
a dedicated link to Google,

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:53.940
it's a good benefit
to be able to use

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:56.730
that link with low latency
access, high throughput,

00:12:56.730 --> 00:12:59.340
to be able to store data to,
let's say, a Cloud Storage

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:01.980
bucket or to load data
into BigQuery services.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:06.060
So it can actually
advertise by a VIP range

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.190
back to your on-premise
environment with that API

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:09.150
that you can call.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:10.650
And then you can
actually make calls

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:13.770
to Google Cloud
API services that

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:15.370
are available through this API.

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:16.953
So this supports
Google API endpoints.

00:13:16.953 --> 00:13:18.453
There's a subset
of endpoints that's

00:13:18.453 --> 00:13:19.720
currently supported with this.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:21.637
And essentially, on-premise,
what you would do

00:13:21.637 --> 00:13:25.590
is you'd do a DNS change to
rewrite the Google API's domain

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:28.710
to use a specific
VIP, a VIP range, that

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:31.380
allows you to connect
to these prefixes

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:32.700
through an interconnect link.

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:35.280
And you would advertise
that prefix, which,

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:41.160
as you can see in this example
here, it's 199.36.153.4/30,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.555
on the Cloud Router back to
your on-premise environment.

00:13:43.555 --> 00:13:45.180
So the Cloud Router
actually allows you

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.400
to program which routes you
would want to advertise.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.950
And by advertising this range
making the necessary DNS

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:51.720
changes, you can
actually connect back

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:55.383
to on-prem to Google
API services natively.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:01.930
And let's talk a bit about
high availability topologies.

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:03.880
So one circuit, as
I mentioned earlier,

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.770
does not give you
any kind of SLAs.

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:09.020
To have an SLA, you need to have
two circuits for a redundancy.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.470
So in this case, we have
a co-location facility

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:13.750
which is located in
one metropolitan area.

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:16.640
That metropolitan area would
have two availability zones.

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:19.960
So to have any kind of
SLA, a 39 SLA with us,

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:21.580
you would need to
connect across two

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:23.110
of these metropolitan
availability

00:14:23.110 --> 00:14:25.270
zones in a single metro.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:27.880
So with BGP Graceful
Restart and Cloud Router,

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:31.120
we're able to provide means
of having high availability

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:34.660
across these two zones.

00:14:34.660 --> 00:14:37.570
Now, for customers that
require very high availability,

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.400
we would want to
duplicate that effort

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:41.168
across two metropolitan areas.

00:14:41.168 --> 00:14:43.210
So in this case, you may
have a metropolitan area

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.430
on the east coast, maybe
one on the west coast.

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:47.500
And you would be,
again, connected

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:49.240
across two availability
zones within

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:50.860
these metropolitan areas.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:53.320
And that would provide
you a 49 SLA with us

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:55.180
to connect back to GCP.

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:58.300
And within GCP, you would deploy
a Cloud Router in one region

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:00.040
and a Cloud Router
in the other region

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:03.190
generally as close as possible
to where you are co-located

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:03.940
geographically.

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:04.960
So if it's on the
West Coast, you

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.400
would pick a region
on the West Coast.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.317
And on the East Coast,
you would pick a region

00:15:08.317 --> 00:15:09.285
on the East Coast.

00:15:09.285 --> 00:15:10.660
With the means of
global routing,

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:12.340
as I mentioned
earlier, we're actually

00:15:12.340 --> 00:15:15.230
able to then natively transit
our network in case of failure.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:17.440
So if we were to lose
an entire region,

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:19.570
we're able to route
via the other region

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:21.820
to connect back to your
on-premise environment

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:23.967
via this model of deployment.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:29.930
And now I'd like to introduce
my colleague Sasha on stage

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.210
to talk about high
availability Cloud VPN.

00:15:35.042 --> 00:15:39.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:40.580
SASHA: Hi, I'm Sasha.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:44.540
I'm one of the engineers
at the VPN team.

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:47.150
And if there is one thing
I learned in my years

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:49.820
working for VPN, it
is that reliability

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:52.100
is a critical feature of VPN.

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.340
Because if your connectivity
from your on-prem location

00:15:55.340 --> 00:16:00.150
to GCP is down, you will feel
it across all the organization.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:03.800
Now, the protocol
that we use to run VPN

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:06.410
is the industry
standard called IPsec.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:10.310
It's pretty good when you
consider its encryption

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:11.450
capabilities.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:15.380
But its ability to detect
and route around failures

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:16.980
is not so good.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:22.970
So if anything fails on the
way from GCP to your location,

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:25.370
then your tunnel will be toast.

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:32.720
And we want to build a
more reliable connection.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:37.060
And to do that, the way
is to use redundancy.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:39.580
So you can set up
several tunnels

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:42.310
running in parallel
between the two locations

00:16:42.310 --> 00:16:46.060
and hoping that if one is down,
the other will still be up.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:49.480
So that gives you
some [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:50.830
but that's not enough.

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:54.580
Because if those tunnels
share any component

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.130
on the way from GCP
to your location,

00:16:57.130 --> 00:16:59.410
if there is a fault
in that component,

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:02.140
again, your whole
connection will be down.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:06.130
So not only you need to
provide a redundant connection,

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:08.800
you need to be able
to isolate failure

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:12.400
by making sure that
at no stage, there

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:16.720
is a single point of failure in
which your tunnel is working.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:20.200
Now, us being cloud, this
is virtualized hardware,

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:22.170
so where do you even start?

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:26.210
So to do that, we
develop a new VPN product

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:28.790
called high availability VPN.

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:34.130
And for that, we have a new
kind of cloud VPN gateway,

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:36.090
slightly different
from the old one.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:41.150
And what it does, it provides
you with two interfaces.

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:45.290
And you can think of them as
two different network clouds,

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:49.588
similarly to the
physical network clouds

00:17:49.588 --> 00:17:50.880
you would put on your computer.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:52.905
So if one of them is
burnt out, the other

00:17:52.905 --> 00:17:55.590
is still continuing
to run your traffic.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:56.840
But this is virtual.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.440
So if you have two
tunnels attached

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:03.080
to two of the interfaces,
we will make sure

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:04.490
that they share nothing.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:08.630
They share no line cards,
no network connections,

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:10.490
no machines, no switches.

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:14.330
Actually, being cloud, we are
pretty sure that they will not

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:16.100
even share a data center.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:18.680
So they are pretty much
separated, and if one of them

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:22.570
goes down, the other
is still assured

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:25.080
and very likely to be working.

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:29.930
And then if a further scenario
happens, then a short time

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:32.810
after that, the
system will discover

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:36.170
that one tunnel went unhealthy
and switch all the traffic over

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.220
to the healthy tunnel.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:43.380
Now, how much exactly that gives
you in terms of reliability,

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:44.450
it's a good question.

00:18:44.450 --> 00:18:47.060
It's a function of how
good is the isolation

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:50.480
and how fast is the
process of switching over

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:53.420
from unhealthy tunnel
to the healthy one.

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:56.150
And we did this
analysis for our system.

00:18:56.150 --> 00:18:57.550
And I'm happy to
announce that we

00:18:57.550 --> 00:19:02.270
are going to provide
[? 49s, ?] which is 99.99%

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:05.310
regional availability.

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:09.680
So it means 4 and 1/2 minutes
of maximum monthly downtime,

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:13.880
or 53 minutes yearly.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:16.110
And just a few notes--

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:17.870
so this is a regional product.

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:20.570
You do not need to
go multi-regional

00:19:20.570 --> 00:19:22.460
to have this level
of reliability.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:24.650
Just one region is enough.

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:27.350
You will be able to connect
just as with classic VPN

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:28.530
that we had.

00:19:28.530 --> 00:19:30.150
You'll be able to
connect both GCP

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.830
to GCP, or to your
on-prem locations,

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:35.540
or even to another cloud.

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:39.640
The only requirement
that is mandatory now

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:41.720
and was optional
with classic VPN

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:44.480
is that we need the
VPN on the other side

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:46.160
to support dynamic routing.

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:47.990
If you think of it,
it makes total sense

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:51.400
because you want your system
to be able to failover,

00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:53.660
to switch the traffic
from one path to another.

00:19:53.660 --> 00:19:55.490
So it's natural.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:20:00.500
And one last thing to
notice is that when

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:04.490
you're providing this
redundant connection,

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:08.150
you're actually having
twice as many tunnels

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:12.480
as you are sure to have in case
there is any system failure.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:16.850
So if you watch the
observable capacity

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:20.780
that you have in your bandwidth,
then it will be twice as much

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:23.940
as you would have assured.

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:29.000
And so if there is any failure,
then your available bandwidth

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:31.400
will go down by a factor of 2.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.800
And that's not the
time when you want

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:37.970
to discover that you actually
needed more tunnels than you

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:39.360
have configured.

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:43.100
So to have a consistent
experience of your bandwidth

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:46.400
staying constant all the
time, what we recommend

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:49.850
is to set up a VPN connection
in an active/passive

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:53.570
configuration, meaning that one
of the tunnels has priority.

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:57.740
And all the traffic flows
to it unless that fails,

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:01.760
and all the traffic switches
over to the other tunnel.

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:04.320
With dynamic routing again,
that's easy to set up.

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.170
You just use different
MED values, and that's it.

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:11.360
So to recap, we are
going to provide

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:13.130
[? 49's ?] availability.

00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:15.020
This system is
currently in alpha.

00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:17.900
it's going to launch
to beta next month.

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:21.740
And yeah, here's to more
traffic running more smoothly.

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:23.278
Nick, back to you.

00:21:23.278 --> 00:21:24.236
Thank you.

00:21:24.236 --> 00:21:24.798
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:24.798 --> 00:21:26.631
NICOLAS DE CRISTOFARO:
Thank you, thank you.

00:21:30.950 --> 00:21:32.420
OK.

00:21:32.420 --> 00:21:34.460
So putting all that
into perspective now,

00:21:34.460 --> 00:21:37.220
I will actually go
through a demo that

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.410
looks at a common
enterprise use case, where

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:42.950
we have an enterprise that
starts with a VPN tunnel.

00:21:42.950 --> 00:21:45.650
Here, for example, I
have a customer project.

00:21:45.650 --> 00:21:47.510
I have an IPsec
tunnel connected back

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:49.450
to a customer router on-prem.

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:51.017
I set, as Sasha
was talking about,

00:21:51.017 --> 00:21:52.850
the MED values, which
are important in terms

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:54.590
of traffic engineering
that we're doing

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:56.430
within the interconnect model.

00:21:56.430 --> 00:22:01.750
So in this case, I have a
standard MED value of 100.

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:03.940
And then I would like to
set up another connection.

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:06.270
This other connection would
be a dedicated or partner

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:07.593
interconnect offering.

00:22:07.593 --> 00:22:10.260
And then I don't want traffic to
switch over to this connection.

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:12.170
So I create a
connection, create it

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:16.340
with a lower MED value of
1,000, and I establish a link.

00:22:16.340 --> 00:22:18.020
Now, once that link
is established,

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:20.360
traffic will not
seamlessly switch over yet

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:22.070
because the value of
the MED priorities

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:24.590
are preferred over
the VPN tunnel,

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:28.930
then from the
interconnect connection.

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:32.130
Now, to do a traffic
switchover, it's very simple.

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:35.688
We just switched the MED values
between the VPN connection

00:22:35.688 --> 00:22:37.730
and the interconnect
connection by swapping them.

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:39.890
And then the traffic
seamlessly moves over.

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:42.980
So now I have a VPN connection
that can be used as a backup.

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:44.913
I can keep it online
in case I need it.

00:22:44.913 --> 00:22:46.580
But right now, my
traffic would actually

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:50.060
be routed across a dedicated
interconnect or partner

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:51.140
interconnect link.

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.470
So here's the topology of what
we're doing for this demo.

00:22:56.470 --> 00:23:00.268
So I actually have a HA VPN
deployed with two interfaces.

00:23:00.268 --> 00:23:02.310
They're both deployed in
active/passive right now

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:05.130
with MED value of
100, MED value of 200,

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.268
so active/passive
on the VPN link.

00:23:07.268 --> 00:23:08.810
And then I have my
interconnect links

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:12.397
with higher MED values,
1,000 and 1,200.

00:23:12.397 --> 00:23:14.730
And they're connected back
to an on-premise environment,

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:17.670
which I'm running a ping
test between that and a VM

00:23:17.670 --> 00:23:19.830
within GCP.

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:21.557
So you can see
here I have-- this

00:23:21.557 --> 00:23:23.140
is my Cloud Console
with this project.

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:25.160
I just showed the topology.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:29.190
So we have here the interconnect
session and the VPN sessions.

00:23:29.190 --> 00:23:31.890
You can see here the 1,000 and
the 1,200 as advertised route

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:32.920
priority.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:37.020
And we can see here the 100
and 200 for my VPN connections,

00:23:37.020 --> 00:23:38.160
my VPN tunnels.

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:47.730
So here, I'm running a ping
test between an on-prem workload

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:49.690
and a VM and GCP.

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:52.680
As you can see here, we
have kind of sporadic pings

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:54.480
between 20 to 30 milliseconds.

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:56.080
This is running
across the internet.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:57.180
This is a VPN tunnel.

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:59.760
It's unpredictable due to the
path nature of the internet.

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:01.918
So I can't really
rely on some guarantee

00:24:01.918 --> 00:24:02.960
in terms of milliseconds.

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:04.000
It's pretty sporadic.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:05.580
It's flipping up and down.

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:07.170
I want to now
switch traffic over

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:10.950
to a dedicated interconnect
link to have higher throughput

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:14.072
and also lower latency.

00:24:14.072 --> 00:24:16.280
So here, I'm just running
a script, which is actually

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:17.960
going to set the
priorities, swap them

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:22.455
between the interconnect
and the VPN tunnels.

00:24:22.455 --> 00:24:24.830
So really what I'm doing is
I'm switching the priorities,

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:28.100
both on the VPN side in GCP--

00:24:28.100 --> 00:24:29.780
both VPN interconnect
side on GCP

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:31.850
and also on-prem from
my on-prem router

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:33.980
to make sure that we're
updating on both sides

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:35.510
so that it's
symmetrical traffic.

00:24:35.510 --> 00:24:38.930
Of course, it's important to
do that so that traffic runs

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:42.530
both symmetrically
between the peers.

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:44.227
And once that's
actually completed,

00:24:44.227 --> 00:24:45.560
then we should see that traffic.

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:47.810
The latency is going to be
reduced quite significantly

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:51.590
because we're going to go across
a dedicated interconnect link

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:53.600
with much lower latency
and higher throughput.

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:56.210
And as you can see now, we've
actually lowered it down.

00:24:56.210 --> 00:24:58.520
Once it stabilizes,
it should be around 8,

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.780
9 minutes milliseconds
when traffic is running

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:02.780
across the interconnect.

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:05.010
As you can see now, it's
around 9 milliseconds.

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:09.047
So that traffic seamlessly moved
over to the interconnect link,

00:25:09.047 --> 00:25:10.130
instead of the VPN tunnel.

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:17.685
And as you can see,
I've seamlessly

00:25:17.685 --> 00:25:18.560
transitioned traffic.

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:19.790
There's no packet loss.

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:20.367
Easy to do.

00:25:20.367 --> 00:25:21.950
Now you can move
traffic over, and you

00:25:21.950 --> 00:25:24.480
have traffic riding across
the interconnect link.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:34.810
And if we look at the Cloud
Console, and we refresh it,

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:37.030
now we see that the
priorities have been swapped,

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:39.160
so the interconnect session
now has 100 and 200.

00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:41.260
That's the preferred
path, active/passive path

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:43.073
across the
interconnect, and 1,000,

00:25:43.073 --> 00:25:45.190
1,200 for the VPN tunnels.

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:47.590
So they're still there in
case we need it as a backup.

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:49.040
In case there
would be a failure,

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:52.160
we can still transition traffic
across the interconnect.

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.820
So it's that simple to be able
to do a migration between VPN

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:57.250
and interconnect seamlessly
between your enterprise

00:25:57.250 --> 00:25:59.170
and GCP.

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.940
And with that, that
is the end of my demo.

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:04.960
I'd like to introduce Steve
Olson from The Home Depot

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:06.850
and talk about his
interconnect experience.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:07.350
Thank you.

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:07.842
STEVE OLSON: Thank you.

00:26:07.842 --> 00:26:08.967
NICOLAS DE CRISTOFARO: Yep.

00:26:08.967 --> 00:26:11.286
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:15.942 --> 00:26:17.400
STEVE OLSON: Hey,
my name is Steve.

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:20.418
But everyone calls me Steve
O. You'll have to excuse me.

00:26:20.418 --> 00:26:22.460
My voice is a little bit
under the weather today.

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:25.850
I caught something here,
but we will push forward.

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:30.230
I've been with The Home
Depot for 12, 13 years, which

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:31.820
means I'm incredibly
tired, and I've

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:33.620
seen lots of different things.

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:36.490
We like to say we've
seen a lot of changes

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:38.540
in Home Depot in
the last five years,

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:42.130
probably more than we've
seen in the past 10 or 15.

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:44.630
And that really goes to speak
to our digital transformation,

00:26:44.630 --> 00:26:45.980
things that we've done.

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:48.107
We've really put IT first.

00:26:48.107 --> 00:26:49.190
It wasn't always the case.

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:51.830
When I first started, IT
was kind of a backseat.

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:53.385
Now IT's kind of a
front-seat driver.

00:26:53.385 --> 00:26:55.760
And we've really done this
strategy called interconnected

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:56.720
retail.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:58.640
And it's really an
omnichannel strategy

00:26:58.640 --> 00:27:02.450
that lets our customers buy
online, pick up in store,

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:05.312
buy online, ship to their
home, buy in the store.

00:27:05.312 --> 00:27:06.770
All of that kind
of merges together

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:08.130
to give the customer the choice.

00:27:08.130 --> 00:27:13.280
And one of the big strategies
that we played with first

00:27:13.280 --> 00:27:14.480
was the cloud.

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:18.140
GCP we chose to do
e-comm for, and we

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:20.278
have a journey that
kind of uses the tools

00:27:20.278 --> 00:27:21.820
that the guys have
been talking about

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:23.327
and how we connect it to GCP.

00:27:23.327 --> 00:27:25.160
But first, I'm going
to start with some base

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.050
facts about Home Depot.

00:27:27.050 --> 00:27:28.700
I won't read everything
on this slide,

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:31.610
but Home Depot was founded 1978.

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:35.900
Last year, we did
$108 billion in sales.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.920
Dot com was up 22%
in sales, and there's

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:42.410
some dot com guys here
I'll put in front.

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:45.030
I think in the last
five years, six years,

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:46.640
we've actually
increased dot com sales

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:49.290
by a billion dollars
almost every single year.

00:27:49.290 --> 00:27:52.790
A lot of that growth has
been in our cloud space.

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:54.495
We completely
replatformed dot com,

00:27:54.495 --> 00:27:56.120
we'll talk about that
in just a second.

00:27:56.120 --> 00:27:59.120
We have over 150 supply
chain facilities.

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:00.950
We have over 2,000
stores spread out

00:28:00.950 --> 00:28:04.130
across Canada, the
US, and Mexico.

00:28:04.130 --> 00:28:06.568
And of course,
400,000 associates.

00:28:06.568 --> 00:28:07.985
What a lot of
people don't realize

00:28:07.985 --> 00:28:10.130
is we do have a big IT presence.

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:12.670
We have over 3,000
IT associates.

00:28:12.670 --> 00:28:15.900
We hired just under 1,000 last
year, and we're still going.

00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:18.910
We have offices all across the
country from our various brands

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:21.890
to define Home Depot and
companies that we use.

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:24.980
And with that, I'll start
with our actual strategy

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:28.440
and how we started
with the cloud.

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:30.920
So what kind of led up
to a distributed model

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:34.550
for us is really just starting
with e-commerce first.

00:28:34.550 --> 00:28:39.620
And around 2014, we completely
re-platformed the website.

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:43.100
And it was decided that we
would go from an on-prem model

00:28:43.100 --> 00:28:44.802
to in the cloud.

00:28:44.802 --> 00:28:46.760
And some of the reasons
are the classic reasons

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:47.690
that you would hear.

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:48.350
Right?

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:50.330
We were building our
on-premise infrastructure

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:53.600
for sales that would be
around Thanksgiving or Black

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:55.550
Friday or Father's Day.

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:57.650
And you'd build this
infrastructure to the point

00:28:57.650 --> 00:29:00.110
where you're using it
for those days only.

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:02.760
And then the rest of the
year, it sits stagnant.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:05.240
So we really wanted a more
on-demand infrastructure

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:06.350
that could scale.

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:08.830
And of course, the
cloud came into that.

00:29:08.830 --> 00:29:10.142
It took multiple years to do.

00:29:10.142 --> 00:29:12.350
And really, this is going
to be a condensed timeline,

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:14.510
but I'll explain exactly
how we got there.

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:17.120
One of the first things
is data movement.

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:20.778
How do we actually get data from
our on-prem to the GCP cloud?

00:29:20.778 --> 00:29:22.820
And the first thing, of
course, is before we even

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:25.690
get going with lots of projects,
is just the basic internet.

00:29:25.690 --> 00:29:28.130
So we had basic
proxies and internet.

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:29.833
And that doesn't
scale incredibly well.

00:29:29.833 --> 00:29:31.250
What if you need
private services?

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:33.470
Just like they talked about
in the slides before--

00:29:33.470 --> 00:29:36.230
VPNs and interconnects.

00:29:36.230 --> 00:29:40.010
We first started using
VPNs, and I've kind of

00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:42.560
got a love/hate
relationship with VPNs.

00:29:42.560 --> 00:29:43.790
Some people like them.

00:29:43.790 --> 00:29:45.080
I hate VPNs.

00:29:45.080 --> 00:29:47.430
And I'll explain that
for a few reasons.

00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:48.290
I mean, VPNs suck.

00:29:48.290 --> 00:29:49.250
Let's face it.

00:29:49.250 --> 00:29:52.400
But if that's all you can
do, that's all you can do.

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:54.073
We actually use static routes.

00:29:54.073 --> 00:29:55.740
There was no Cloud
Router at this point.

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:56.920
This was around 2015.

00:29:56.920 --> 00:30:00.130
So we were stuck with static
routes and no redundancy.

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:03.860
And it worked, except it
terminated on-prem here,

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:06.350
and then it terminated
inside the cloud.

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:09.020
It basically did
what it needed to do.

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:10.520
But we needed
something more robust.

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:14.208
And we needed to scale
higher And so the problem

00:30:14.208 --> 00:30:16.250
is with VPN, which is
something we'll talk about.

00:30:16.250 --> 00:30:19.190
MTU-- how many of you have ever
dealt with frame size issues

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:20.530
and MTU problems with VPNs?

00:30:20.530 --> 00:30:22.730
It's like yeah, everyone's
hands going up, right?

00:30:22.730 --> 00:30:24.290
MTU is a terrible
thing to deal with.

00:30:24.290 --> 00:30:26.810
And it's not Google's
fault. But Google, you

00:30:26.810 --> 00:30:29.360
have to have fragmentation
before encapsulation.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:33.170
And if you ever dealt with
firewalls, and packet captures,

00:30:33.170 --> 00:30:35.480
and wire captures for why
something doesn't work,

00:30:35.480 --> 00:30:37.580
does your application even
support fragmentation?

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:40.028
Does it know it's
going to be fragmented?

00:30:40.028 --> 00:30:41.820
I just need an Advil
just talking about it.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:44.930
We dealt with that so long that
we needed to get rid of that.

00:30:44.930 --> 00:30:47.268
Speed and latency is
another big thing.

00:30:47.268 --> 00:30:49.685
One of the big things that I
found about speed and latency

00:30:49.685 --> 00:30:51.690
is that if you talk
to your customers,

00:30:51.690 --> 00:30:54.273
sometimes they don't know what
the difference between that is.

00:30:54.273 --> 00:30:56.100
They might say, I
need something quick,

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:57.740
but does quick
mean I need to load

00:30:57.740 --> 00:30:59.570
a terabyte of data in a day?

00:30:59.570 --> 00:31:01.882
Or I need a connection to a
database in 5 milliseconds?

00:31:01.882 --> 00:31:04.340
Sometimes you have to actually
lead that conversation there

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:06.560
to see exactly what
they care about.

00:31:06.560 --> 00:31:09.380
All right, SLA and the
internet in general,

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:10.782
that's a big thing as well.

00:31:10.782 --> 00:31:13.240
You come in one day and your
connection across the internet

00:31:13.240 --> 00:31:15.267
is 30 milliseconds, and
the next day, it's 45.

00:31:15.267 --> 00:31:16.350
We've all dealt with that.

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:19.990
We actually made a diagram that
basically shows cold potato,

00:31:19.990 --> 00:31:22.800
hot potato routing to explain
to our application developers

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:25.410
and our consumers what
it's like that, yes, we

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:27.840
have internet
connections to the cloud,

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:29.620
but we don't go straight there.

00:31:29.620 --> 00:31:31.642
I don't own the
infrastructure in the middle.

00:31:31.642 --> 00:31:33.600
And getting their heads
wrapped around the fact

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:35.700
that yes, I may
leave here, but I

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:37.890
don't know what some
tier one provider does

00:31:37.890 --> 00:31:39.280
with it after the fact.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.960
And that's how we
explain that to them.

00:31:42.960 --> 00:31:45.170
So let's deal with
the VPN issue first.

00:31:45.170 --> 00:31:49.070
So we really wanted to have
a way that the VPN be better.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:50.790
We couldn't fix the
MTU immediately,

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:53.280
but we could fix some of the
speed and latency problems.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:54.697
So this is where
we really started

00:31:54.697 --> 00:31:56.430
discussing peering facilities.

00:31:56.430 --> 00:31:58.850
So we actually
came up with, if we

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:01.340
could get the VPNs
closer to Google,

00:32:01.340 --> 00:32:03.020
we can actually get
performance better--

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:06.840
so speed, latency,
reliability, SLA.

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:09.740
All right, so we came up with
peering centers on the East

00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:12.440
and on the West Coast.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:14.680
Those are the first good
choices because a lot

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.180
of the infrastructure for things
that we're using are there.

00:32:17.180 --> 00:32:18.847
And we have well-defined
lanes used now.

00:32:18.847 --> 00:32:20.610
So instead of
using the internet,

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:22.955
we own these private circuits
between some of our data

00:32:22.955 --> 00:32:25.130
centers directly to
these peering centers.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:28.250
So I can tell my application
developers and all my teams,

00:32:28.250 --> 00:32:30.920
I know what you're going to
get from a latency perspective.

00:32:30.920 --> 00:32:32.420
I know this is 15.

00:32:32.420 --> 00:32:33.950
I know this is 40.

00:32:33.950 --> 00:32:36.480
And we don't have to
worry about the internet.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:39.410
So what we really did is--
revisiting the first slide--

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:43.380
is we moved the VPN connections
all the way out to the colo

00:32:43.380 --> 00:32:45.050
there in the peering centers.

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:46.710
And the VPN's still
inside the project.

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:49.280
So the internet is
not used, right?

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:52.250
But reliability
and SLA go way up.

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:54.300
Speed is now
actually even better,

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:56.220
because now we have
direct connections.

00:32:56.220 --> 00:32:58.485
So we're running the VPN
basically on the last mile.

00:32:58.485 --> 00:33:00.110
We're not running it
across the country

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:01.370
and hoping that it gets there.

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:04.110
We have our own private
circuits that gets us there.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:07.100
So the real redundancy
actually still isn't here yet.

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:10.910
So we actually started
doing multiple VPNs,

00:33:10.910 --> 00:33:12.890
but without Cloud Router,
and static routes,

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:14.680
and trying to scale that.

00:33:14.680 --> 00:33:16.100
Yeah, that didn't work either.

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:19.220
So we needed to do something
even better than that.

00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:21.440
It actually worked well
enough that we basically

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:22.760
started adding more.

00:33:22.760 --> 00:33:25.210
People liked it so much
that we added a couple more.

00:33:25.210 --> 00:33:27.240
So now we have 7
milliseconds and 3.

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:30.050
And we actually have vendors
and application owners

00:33:30.050 --> 00:33:32.330
that test this and can get
connections straight to GCP

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:33.740
within 10 milliseconds.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:36.920
All right, and so once we
started doing that, we thought,

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:38.720
well, interconnect beta started.

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:40.490
And if we could get
the interconnect on,

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:42.600
we could get rid of the VPNs.

00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:46.090
So now we're going
to fix the VPN issue.

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:47.740
That's still a
problem with the MTU.

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:50.330
So what happens is the
interconnect beta comes,

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:53.180
Cloud Router comes, and we
have now an interconnect

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:54.550
plus a direct connect.

00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:56.140
So at each of the
peering facilities,

00:33:56.140 --> 00:33:57.390
we have four different cables.

00:33:57.390 --> 00:34:00.680
We have two for public
access, two for interconnect,

00:34:00.680 --> 00:34:02.540
and depending on what
the project needs are,

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:03.998
they can do private
access, or they

00:34:03.998 --> 00:34:06.410
can do public access over
our own infrastructure

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:06.980
to get there.

00:34:06.980 --> 00:34:08.880
The internet's not involved.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:10.580
And of course, with
dynamic routing,

00:34:10.580 --> 00:34:12.290
the redundancy is
much more seamless.

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:15.290
And it fails over much easier.

00:34:15.290 --> 00:34:18.250
So with that, we
basically grew even more.

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:20.949
We actually added another
facility this year.

00:34:20.949 --> 00:34:22.750
And all these slides
back each other up.

00:34:22.750 --> 00:34:25.600
They were talking about
mission critical reliability.

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:28.179
We built this in such a way
that when we have circuit issues

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:30.969
and some of these go down, our
application orders don't even

00:34:30.969 --> 00:34:31.947
notice.

00:34:31.947 --> 00:34:34.030
Because if they're
multi-region and multi-attached

00:34:34.030 --> 00:34:36.969
to different interconnects
and use Cloud Routers,

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:40.320
it automatically fails over.

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:44.110
We've scaled to there now,
but we're even adding more.

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:46.330
And really, it's
not just GCP either.

00:34:46.330 --> 00:34:49.060
The journey to actually getting
us connected directly to GCP

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:50.380
led us to other things.

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:52.130
We're using it for
other providers as well

00:34:52.130 --> 00:34:54.588
and other vendors and partners
of us, and even the internet

00:34:54.588 --> 00:34:55.300
itself.

00:34:55.300 --> 00:34:57.670
So just the concept
of getting to GCP

00:34:57.670 --> 00:35:00.190
let us to this distributed
regionalized network

00:35:00.190 --> 00:35:01.540
that we can use.

00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:04.750
And with the combination
of new technologies

00:35:04.750 --> 00:35:07.330
like SD WAN and
things like that, we

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:09.583
can have our remote
sites, our stores,

00:35:09.583 --> 00:35:11.500
our distribution centers,
we can have them all

00:35:11.500 --> 00:35:13.440
come to the closest
peering facility

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:16.180
and use Cloud Services, or
use the internet, and things

00:35:16.180 --> 00:35:17.198
that they might need.

00:35:17.198 --> 00:35:19.240
They don't need to come
back to the main offices.

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:20.740
They need to go directly
to the internet.

00:35:20.740 --> 00:35:22.910
We can control it all in a
centralized, regionalized

00:35:22.910 --> 00:35:25.440
manner.

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:28.560
And so, with that being
said, what we're doing next

00:35:28.560 --> 00:35:30.732
is we're investigating
what they talked about

00:35:30.732 --> 00:35:31.690
in the last few slides.

00:35:31.690 --> 00:35:35.130
It is private services
via these channels,

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:37.070
meaning using our
private space to contact

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:39.220
BigQuery and things like that.

00:35:39.220 --> 00:35:41.460
We're really interested
in shared VPC,

00:35:41.460 --> 00:35:43.920
and we're going to continue
deploying more peering centers

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:46.440
and keep using the
multi-regional model, and Cloud

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:48.060
Routers, and interconnects.

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:50.310
And we'll probably even
expand to more peering centers

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:52.060
in Canada and Mexico this year.

00:35:52.060 --> 00:35:53.700
This model's proved
extremely well.

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:55.050
Our customers like it.

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:56.620
And we will continue to do that.

00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:59.020
With that being said,
that is my talk.

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:02.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

